3. Thematic synthesis (short)
- Process fundamentals: ISHF couples incremental deformation by a localized punch with fluid backing (hydrostatic support) to improve formability and reduce thinning compared to dry ISF. Key variables: toolpath strategy, step depth, feed rate, tool diameter, lubrication, and hydrostatic pressure/support.
- Materials & forming limits: Aluminum alloys (1xxx, 3xxx, 5xxx series) widely studied; high-strength steels and difficult-to-form alloys require heated or hybrid approaches. ISHF extends forming limits relative to dry ISF, but limits depend strongly on process parameters and support configuration.
- FEM & modeling: Advances include explicit dynamic FEM for large deformation, coupled CFD‑structural models for fluid‑solid interaction in hydroforming support, and multiscale crystal plasticity to predict strain localization and forming limits.
- Process control & optimization: DOE (Taguchi, RSM), machine learning (ANN) and closed‑loop force/position control reduce defects and improve repeatability. Adaptive toolpath strategies and variable step depth are effective.
- Gaps and challenges: Standardized forming limit datasets for ISHF are limited; more coupled CFD‑FEM experimental validation needed; scale-up and cycle time/automation for industrial adoption are open issues.

4. Practical recommendations for researchers and designers
- For aluminum ISHF, prioritize experimentation on hydraulic backing pressure and toolpath optimization; use Taguchi or RSM designs to identify dominant parameters (step depth, feed rate).
- Use coupled CFD‑FEM or at least calibrated hydrostatic support models when simulating ISHF; consider multiscale material models for high‑strain predictions.
- Implement closed‑loop force or position control to manage tool–sheet interaction and prevent defects; consider real‑time thickness estimation via process sensors for adaptive control.
- For hard‑to‑form materials, explore localized heating (induction, friction) combined with hydrostatic support to extend formability.

